rms learning. So no, I’m not literally agreeing with what you’ve been saying this whole time—because what you’ve been saying (or at least what your colleague said, which you’ve been defending) is the strong version: “men act unintelligently to the point that we should simplify training for them as a gender-based rule.” I can’t get there, and it’s not because I’m dodging; it’s because that conclusion doesn’t follow, even from the real patterns we both observe. If you want me to say: “Yes, I see why you’d be frustrated, and no, you’re not crazy for noticing that many men do things that look dumb on a regular basis”—I’ll say that without hesitation. But the moment that becomes a training design principle, I’m out, because it’s bad practice and unfair to the individuals in the room. That’s the only line I’m holding.
